WEBVTT

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:24.830
<v Ira Glass>Here's the kind of thing that changes you from one kind of person into another. Adam and Wendy had been looking for a house for years, which is what you have to do if you don't have a lot of money. And finally, they found this house that they liked. And the broker showed it to them before it came into the market, so the actually had a shot at getting the house. And then, they were completely ripped off, but ripped off in this way that seems almost calculated to get inside their very molecules and rip them off at the cellular level.
<v Ira Glass>这就是那种让你从一种人变成另一种人的事情。亚当和温迪多年来一直在找房子，这是如果你没有很多钱时必须做的。最终，他们找到了这栋他们喜欢的房子。房产经纪人在房子上市前就向他们展示了它，所以他们实际上有机会买下这栋房子。然后，他们完全被坑了，但这种被坑的方式似乎经过精心计算，深入到他们的每一个分子，在细胞层面将他们洗劫一空。

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:31.060
<v Adam>The owners of the house, they were very warm and friendly with us, and--
<v Adam>房子的主人对我们非常热情友好，而且——

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:31.860
<v Wendy>Paternal.
<v Wendy>像长辈一样。

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:40.110
<v Adam>Paternal, they were very paternal. In fact, they reminded me of my parents in a way. They were an academic couple.
<v Adam>像长辈一样，他们非常像长辈。事实上，他们在某种程度上让我想起了我的父母。他们是一对学者夫妻。

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:49.380
<v Wendy>She seemed like an old hippie, which made me feel like, I don't know, she's pretty genuine. She wore clogs and just seemed kind of cool, and long gray hair, and--
<v Wendy>她看起来像个老嬉皮士，这让我觉得，我不知道，她挺真诚的。她穿着木屐，看起来很酷，留着灰色长发，而且——

00:00:49.380 --> 00:01:04.030
<v Adam>They said to us at one point, "You know, we have great compassion for you two because we've got children your own age. And so we have great compassion for what you're doing."
<v Adam>有一次他们对我们说：“你们知道，我们非常同情你们两个，因为我们也有和你们年纪相仿的孩子。所以我们对你们正在做的事情深表同情。”

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:12.540
<v Wendy>But it seemed really genuine at the time.
<v Wendy>但当时这看起来真的很真诚。

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:30.030
<v Ira Glass>An inspector came and pointed out a few problems with the house. For instance, the owners had said that the roof was brand new. But in fact, the inspector said, it had simply been painted silver and was a web of cracks and problems. The steps leading up to the house were being rebuilt, but the inspector said that the workmanship was no good. It would not last the winter.
<v Ira Glass>一位验房师来了，指出了房子的一些问题。例如，房主曾说屋顶是全新的。但实际上，验房师说，它只是被刷成了银色，到处都是裂缝和问题。通往房子的台阶正在重建，但验房师说工艺不好，根本熬不过冬天。

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:43.940
<v Ira Glass>And then there was the question of the abandoned house next door. What was going to happen there? They understood that they knew nothing about it, nothing at all. But might it affect the value of this house? The owners insisted on talking out the problems face-to-face.
<v Ira Glass>还有隔壁那栋废弃房子的事。那儿会发生什么？他们声称对此一无所知，完全不知道。但这会影响这栋房子的价值吗？房主坚持要当面谈这些问题。

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:48.200
<v Wendy>And they invited us over and gave us coffee. And we sat right in this room.
<v Wendy>他们邀请我们过去，还给了我们咖啡。我们就坐在现在的这个房间里。

00:01:48.200 --> 00:02:06.300
<v Adam>We sat right here. I think we sat in their couch, which was where our couch now is. And we looked them in the eye, and they sat across from us. And that's when I got the first sense that they were playing us.
<v Adam>我们就坐在这儿。我想我们当时坐在他们的沙发上，也就是我们现在放沙发的地方。我们直视他们的眼睛，他们坐在我们对面。就在那时，我第一次感觉到他们是在耍我们。

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:13.970
<v Wendy>They didn't believe the inspector. They said, that's crazy, we have a new roof. And this guy that is doing the stoop is doing a very good job in fact.
<v Wendy>他们不相信验房师。他们说，那太荒谬了，我们的屋顶是新的。而且这个修台阶的人事实上干得非常好。

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:31.240
<v Adam>They made the point that the roof wasn't leaking on them. Therefore, it was not, as far as they were concerned, a problem. Therefore, why should it really enter the negotiations?
<v Adam>他们强调说屋顶并没有漏水。因此，在他们看来，这就不是问题。所以，为什么要把它纳入谈判呢？

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:47.370
<v Ira Glass>Negotiations went back and forth for weeks. Wendy and Adam barely got their way on anything. But they bought the house anyway because they knew that if they didn't, somebody else would. They move in. And then, the very first time that it rains, streams of brown water come pouring into the house.
<v Ira Glass>谈判进行了好几个星期。温迪和亚当几乎什么都没谈成。但他们还是买了这栋房子，因为他们知道如果不买，别人就会买走。他们搬了进去。然后，第一次下雨时，一股股棕色的水就涌进了屋里。

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:55.990
<v Wendy>So the water started dripping from there, where you can see the brown stains. Water was coming down here.
<v Wendy>所以水开始从那里滴下来，你可以看到棕色的污渍。水就是从这里流下来的。

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:02.180
<v Adam>The wall was bubbling out with a big pocket of water inside, and the plaster was falling.
<v Adam>墙壁鼓起一个大水泡，里面的灰泥正在脱落。

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:19.800
<v Wendy>I just felt totally defeated when I saw that water. I couldn't believe it, how they just kept saying, the roof is new, you guys are crazy. You're totally overreacting. You can't listen to contractors who tell you you need a new roof. Yeah, you need a new roof. Yeah, right. Well, we've never had this. This place has never-- we never had a leak.
<v Wendy>看到那水的时候，我感到彻底崩溃了。我简直不敢相信，他们竟然一直说，屋顶是新的，你们疯了吧。你们反应过度了。你们不能听信那些告诉你们需要换屋顶的承包商的话。是啊，你需要换屋顶。是啊，没门。好吧，我们从来没遇到过这种情况。这个地方从来没——我们从来没漏过水。

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:43.660
<v Wendy>And as soon as the water started, I started to really look at the ceiling. And I noticed that, wait a minute, you can see where it was leaking before. I had never noticed. It was very subtle, just a little bit of yellowing. And someone had repaired it. So they did lie to us.
<v Wendy>水一漏，我就开始仔细观察天花板。我注意到，等一下，你可以看到它之前漏过的地方。我以前从没注意到。非常细微，只有一点点泛黄。有人修补过它。所以他们确实对我们撒了谎。

00:03:43.660 --> 00:04:15.120
<v Ira Glass>The second day they were in the house, Adam was walking up the steps to the front door, and he heard a sound, the steps cracking under his feet. Their first weekend in the house, they woke up to a different sound, the sound of jackhammers. It turns out that not only was there construction starting on the house next door, it would extend that house 15 feet back, blocking out all of the western sunlight from Adam and Wendy's backyard. And if that were not enough, neighbors informed them that the previous owners knew all about it, in fact, had led an organizing drive on the block to stop this construction.
<v Ira Glass>他们住进房子的第二天，亚当走上台阶去前门，听到了一声响，那是台阶在他脚下开裂的声音。他们在房子里的第一个周末，被另一种声音吵醒了，是手提钻的声音。原来，隔壁不仅开始动工，还要将房子向后扩建15英尺，挡住了亚当和温迪后院所有的西向阳光。如果这还不够，邻居告诉他们，前房主对此一清二楚，事实上，他们还曾在街区组织过请愿活动来阻止这项施工。

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:27.580
<v Ira Glass>And what makes the whole thing so galling is they're still living in the home of the people who took advantage of them. Every day, they're reminded of these people. They smell their smell in the closet. When the drains back up, it's their hair and grease.
<v Ira Glass>最让人气愤的是，他们仍然住在那些占了他们便宜的人的房子里。每天，他们都会想起这些人。他们在壁橱里闻到他们的气味。当排水管堵塞时，那是他们的头发和油脂。

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:39.120
<v Adam>We've gone through the house, over all the surfaces with razor blades, scraping off the fingerprints and the dirt. And it's obsessive. We've gotten overly meticulous about it.
<v Adam>我们已经用剃须刀片刮遍了屋里的所有表面，刮掉了指纹和污垢。这是一种强迫行为。我们在这些方面变得过于细致了。

00:04:39.120 --> 00:05:01.370
<v Wendy>But every time I open those doors over there, I think of them. I think of their greasy hands, every time, even though I have scrubbed. In fact, over here, you can see I became really crazy, and I started scrubbing these cabinets. And I actually got the finish off of it, I was scrubbing so hard.
<v Wendy>但每次我打开那边的门，我就会想到他们。我就会想到他们油腻的手，即使我已经刷洗过了。事实上，在这边，你可以看到我变得很疯狂，我开始擦洗这些橱柜。我擦得太用力，竟然把漆面都刷掉了。

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:29.160
<v Ira Glass>Now, Wendy and Adam, I should say, are the sort of do-gooders who, when they move out of an apartment they've been renting, they'll hire a cleaner to make sure that the place is spotless for the next tenants. When they sell a car, they get that extra brake job for $600 before putting it on the market, not because they necessarily think it'll get a better price, and usually it does not, but because they think that it is right. They believe in deals where everybody walks away feeling like the deal was fair. Or anyway, that's the kind of people they used to be before they bought a house.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我得说，温迪和亚当属于那种好好先生，当他们从租住的公寓搬走时，会请清洁工确保房子对下一任租客来说一尘不染。当他们卖车时，会在上市前多花600美元去换刹车片，不是因为他们觉得这样能卖出更好的价钱（通常也不会），而是因为他们觉得这样做才是对的。他们相信那种双方都能对交易感到公平的交易。或者说，在买这栋房子之前，他们是那种人。

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:37.930
<v Adam>That's changed the way I deal with people now. I'm bitter about it. I don't trust anyone anymore.
<v Adam>现在，这改变了我对待他人的方式。我对此感到愤恨。我再也不信任任何人了。

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.600
<v Ira Glass>In every negotiation, the way you see it now is, somebody's going to be the sucker.
<v Ira Glass>在每一次谈判中，你现在的看法就是，总有人会当冤大头。

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:46.680
<v Adam>Yeah, I feel that way now. I feel that there's a winner and a loser.
<v Adam>是的，我现在有这种感觉。我觉得世界上有赢家和输家。

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:53.430
<v Wendy>That you're either going to get screwed by somebody, or you're screwing somebody.
<v Wendy>要么就是你被别人坑，要么就是你坑别人。

00:05:53.430 --> 00:06:09.870
<v Ira Glass>Just last weekend, they went to a big home supplies hardware chain, a chain where they have gotten terrible advice in the past, terrible service. They needed some bolts. And, filled with anger and a spirit of vengeance, they simply stole them. The opportunity presented itself. They took advantage.
<v Ira Glass>就在上周末，他们去了一家大型家居五金连锁店，一家他们过去曾获得过糟糕建议、体验过糟糕服务的连锁店。他们需要一些螺栓。心中充满了愤怒和报复心理，他们直接把螺栓偷走了。机会摆在眼前。他们利用了它。

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:21.430
<v Ira Glass>And my friend, it felt good to steal. It felt good to make somebody else the sucker. They think they're going to do it again. That is what they have learned from the previous owners of their house.
<v Ira Glass>我的朋友，偷窃的感觉真好。让别人当冤大头的感觉真好。他们认为他们还会再干一次。这就是他们从这栋房子的前房主那里学到的。

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:48.270
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it is This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. Today on our program, Suckers. There are some people who see the world as a place where everybody can work out their differences and come to settlements, where everybody is happy, everybody getting along. And there are people who believe it's eat or be eaten, screw or be screwed. Today, the chasm that divides those two worldviews.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。今天我们节目的主题是：冤大头。有些人认为世界是一个大家可以解决分歧、达成和解、人人快乐、和睦相处的地方。还有些人认为这是一个弱肉强食、要么坑人要么被坑的世界。今天，我们要探讨分隔这两种世界观的鸿沟。

00:06:48.270 --> 00:07:02.560
<v Ira Glass>They both can't be right. Either the world is dog-eat-dog or not. Today, we try to figure out which it is. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>它们不可能都对。世界要么是弱肉强食的，要么不是。今天，我们试图找出真相。请继续收听。

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:26.420
<v Ira Glass>Act One, No Receipt, No Surrender. Perhaps you're familiar with the story of a certain New Jersey family. They live in a lovely suburban home. The neighbors have no idea how all the nice stuff they own arrives there, or if they suspect, they keep their mouths shut. The attitude of the head of the household, sometimes there are opportunities to take, and you take them. Or maybe you don't know the family I'm talking about.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：没收据，不退让。也许你听说过新泽西州某个家庭的故事。他们住在一个漂亮的郊区住宅里。邻居们不知道他们拥有的那些好东西是怎么来的，即使他们有所怀疑，也会守口如瓶。这家之主的态度是，有时有些机会可以把握，你就去把握。又或者，你不知道我说的是哪一家。

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:30.850
<v Sheila>I wouldn't even call it a crime. People do it all the time.
<v Sheila>我甚至不会称之为犯罪。人们经常这么做。

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:46.410
<v Ira Glass>This is a woman who asked to be identified only by her first name, Sheila, for reasons that will become all too clear to you. I should say that she does not make a living off of her little random opportunities. Her husband is a doctor, and he supports the family. But she embodies the kind of worldview that we're talking about in this week's show.
<v Ira Glass>这位女士要求只以她的名字“希拉”来称呼，原因你很快就会明白。我应该说，她并不是靠这些随机的小机会为生。她丈夫是位医生，支撑着这个家庭。但她体现了我们这期节目要探讨的那种世界观。

00:07:46.410 --> 00:08:06.350
<v Ira Glass>She's the sort of person who talks hotels into giving her discounts when, really, they have no reason to, who shows up at famous restaurants where she has no reservation, sneaks a look at the names in the reservation book, and then claims to be one of the people listed in order to be seated right away. She's talked her way into sold-out events. She's talked her way into the Getty Museum. Here's a typical scam.
<v Ira Glass>她是那种能说服酒店给她折扣的人，尽管他们根本没理由这么做；是那种在没有预订的情况下出现在著名餐厅，偷偷看一眼预订簿上的名字，然后冒充其中一人以求立刻入座的人。她曾凭三寸不烂之舌进入售罄的活动现场。她还曾凭此进入盖蒂博物馆。这是她典型的骗术之一。

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:26.370
<v Sheila>I had been shopping with my older daughter. And I insisted that she buy this most gorgeous Geoffrey Beene outfit that had the Saks Fifth Avenue hangtag price of, I think, $3,800. And at Loehmann's, it was, oh, about $200. And I said, "Oh, Susan, you really have to have this."
<v Sheila>我和大女儿去购物。我坚持让她买了这套最华丽的杰弗里·比恩套装，我记得萨克斯第五大道百货的吊牌价是3800美元。在Loehmann's百货店，价格大概是200美元。我说：“哦，苏珊，你一定要买下这件。”

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:49.980
<v Sheila>OK, well, she took it, and, of course, as luck would have it, she never put it on her back. About a year or so later, I said to my husband, "Would you mind detouring into the city, and we'll go to the Geoffrey Beene boutique at Saks?" And he said, OK. He doesn't generally approve of things like this, but I really didn't tell him what the purpose was.
<v Sheila>好吧，她拿下了它，当然，正如运气所致，她从来没穿过。大约一年左右后，我对丈夫说：“你不介意绕道进城，我们去萨克斯的杰弗里·比恩专卖店吧？”他说，好吧。他通常不赞成这样的事情，但我真的没告诉他目的是什么。

00:08:49.980 --> 00:09:11.120
<v Sheila>So I took the garment upstairs to the boutique, and I spoke to the buyer, told them that this had been purchased at another Saks, and I would like to return it. And he said, "That's not a problem." And he looked at the hangtag, and he said, "Well, the item has been marked down. It's now, I think, about $2,200."
<v Sheila>于是我把衣服带到楼上的精品店，告诉买手这件衣服是在另一家萨克斯买的，我想退货。他说：“没问题。”他看了看吊牌，说：“嗯，这件商品已经降价了。我想现在大概是2200美元。”

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.600
<v Ira Glass>No longer $3,800.
<v Ira Glass>不再是3800美元了。

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.930
<v Sheila>Right. I said, "Well, that's all right. Just credit my account."
<v Sheila>对。我说：“嗯，那没关系。就把钱退到我账户里吧。”

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:21.780
<v Ira Glass>That was a $2,000 profit. You paid $200 for it at Loehmann's.
<v Ira Glass>那可是2000美元的利润。你在Loehmann's才花了200美元。

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.000
<v Sheila>Right, right, right.
<v Sheila>对，对，对。

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:32.420
<v Ira Glass>Now, your daughter Jen is sitting in the studio with you. Jen, do you just want to give your perspective on this, what this was like growing up with this happening all the time?
<v Ira Glass>现在，你女儿珍和你一起在演播室里。珍，你想分享一下你的看法吗？从小到大一直经历这些是什么感觉？

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:50.160
<v Jen>I knew from a very young age that my mother wasn't necessarily like other mothers. My mother had a very large Country Squire station wagon when I was growing up. And oftentimes, the back of the car would be filled with things that were not illegally gotten, but certainly questionably gotten.
<v Jen>我很小的时候就知道我妈妈和其他妈妈不一定一样。我长大时，妈妈有一辆很大的乡村绅士旅行车。通常，车后座堆满了并不是通过合法渠道，但肯定是通过可疑渠道得来的东西。

00:09:50.160 --> 00:10:04.880
<v Jen>I remember very clearly when I was very young, there was a promotion at a bank. And if you opened a checking account, you could get, I think, one blender or one toaster, one of something. But my mother thought, well, why get one, when I could open multiple checking accounts and get a whole bunch of them?
<v Jen>我清楚地记得我很小的时候，有一家银行搞促销。如果你开一个支票账户，可以得到，我想，一个搅拌机或一个烤面包机，或者别的东西。但我妈妈想，既然我可以开多个支票账户并拿到一堆，为什么要拿一个呢？

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:31.120
<v Jen>And I remember returning to our house, I must have been maybe five or six, with the trunk filled with these blenders or toasters. And we already had a blender and a toaster. We didn't need multiples. But my mother has built this warehouse in our basement where she literally outfitted the homes of all of my cousins, who are all older than I am. I don't think anybody bought anything off the shelf.
<v Jen>我记得回到家时，我大概才五六岁，后备箱里装满了这些搅拌机或烤面包机。我们已经有一个搅拌机和一个烤面包机了。我们不需要那么多。但我妈妈在地下室建了一个仓库，她真的把所有堂兄弟姐妹的家都装备齐了，他们都比我大。我不认为有谁是从商店买过东西的。

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.730
<v Ira Glass>How often will you do something like this? Will you have something like this going, say, once a week or so?
<v Ira Glass>你多久会做一次这样的事？比如说，一周左右会有一次这样的情况吗？

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:36.010
<v Sheila>Oh, no.
<v Sheila>哦，不。

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:36.590
<v Jen>Mom?
<v Jen>妈妈？

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:39.190
<v Sheila>No, a few times during the year.
<v Sheila>不，一年也就几次。

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:46.300
<v Jen>Mom, I would say, conservatively, 24 times a year, you've got some scam running.
<v Jen>妈妈，我觉得保守估计，你一年大概有24次在搞这种把戏。

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.650
<v Ira Glass>What else has your mom done that you could never see yourself doing, when it comes to these kinds of things?
<v Ira Glass>关于这些事情，你妈妈还做过什么你觉得自己永远不会做的事？

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.250
<v Jen>Oh, boy.
<v Jen>哦，天哪。

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.230
<v Sheila>The Getty.
<v Sheila>盖蒂博物馆。

00:10:54.230 --> 00:11:22.530
<v Jen>Yeah, the Getty was a big one. My mother and I were at a wedding in California, in Los Angeles, and my mother very much wanted to go to the Getty. And I was keen on going to the Getty, but knew that you have two options. You either make a reservation months in advance to get a parking space, or you park at the bottom of a hill and take a bus up, which seems to me not the worst thing in the world. But my mother says to the attendant, we're on the list. We're staying at the Bel Air, and I believe the concierge made a reservation. I should be on the list. We have a parking space.
<v Jen>对，盖蒂那次是个大手笔。我和妈妈在加州洛杉矶参加婚礼，我妈妈非常想去盖蒂中心。我也很想去，但我知道你有两个选择。你要么提前几个月预约以获得停车位，要么停在山脚下坐公车上去，在我看来这也不是什么大不了的事。但我妈妈对管理员说，我们在名单上。我们住在贝莱尔酒店，我相信礼宾部做了预约。我应该在名单上。我们有停车位。

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:33.600
<v Jen>And clearly, we have not made a reservation. I know we have not made a reservation. And I am livid. And we drive in, and that was a very tense moment.
<v Jen>显然，我们根本没预约。我知道我们没预约。我气疯了。我们开车进去，那一刻非常紧张。

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.540
<v Ira Glass>And then, what are you saying to him to--
<v Ira Glass>然后，你是怎么对他说的——

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:46.350
<v Sheila>Oh, but it's not possible. These reservations were made six months ago. I would not just show up here without a reservation. Do I look like that kind of a person?
<v Sheila>哦，但这是不可能的。这些预约是六个月前做的。我不会就这样在没预约的情况下跑过来。我看起来像那种人吗？

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:52.580
<v Jen>With me rolling my eyes next to her. How he didn't see that and know that this was a scam, I have no idea.
<v Jen>当时我在她旁边翻着白眼。他怎么没看出来这是一场骗局，我真不知道。

00:11:52.580 --> 00:12:05.040
<v Sheila>I happen to look like a very honorable, decent sort of a person. But I have found a way to get around the system, which many people are reluctant to do.
<v Sheila>我碰巧看起来像是一个非常高尚、正派的人。但我已经找到了一种绕过系统的方法，这是许多人不愿意做的。

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.740
<v Ira Glass>And will you just keep going until they give you what you want? Or will you stop at some point?
<v Ira Glass>你会一直纠缠直到他们给你想要的东西吗？还是会在某个时候停下来？

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.690
<v Sheila>Yes, I am very tenacious.
<v Sheila>是的，我非常执着。

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:14.590
<v Ira Glass>And so your method is you just don't stop.
<v Ira Glass>所以你的方法就是不停下来。

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:23.320
<v Sheila>Right, because I won't take no for an answer. I just wear the person down, I suppose. And very often, it doesn't take very long.
<v Sheila>对，因为我不接受“不”作为答案。我想我只是把对方磨垮了。通常情况下，这花不了多长时间。

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:26.470
<v Ira Glass>For you, it's not about the money. It's just that it's like a sport for you, right?
<v Ira Glass>对你来说，这根本不是钱的问题。这更像是一项运动，对吧？

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:53.360
<v Sheila>It definitely is. It certainly is not about the money. My dad was a very successful executive. And he always said to me, if you walk into a place and hold your head up high, nobody will ever question because you look as though you belong where you are. And that has served me in good stead. Wherever I have wanted to get into, I have been successful in getting in.
<v Sheila>绝对是。当然绝对不是为了钱。我爸爸是一位非常成功的经理人。他总是对我说，如果你走进一个地方，昂首挺胸，没人会质疑你，因为你看上去就属于那里。这对我很有帮助。无论我想去哪里，我都能成功进入。

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.330
<v Ira Glass>Jen, do you sort of go too far in the other direction?
<v Ira Glass>珍，你是不是在另一个方向走得太远了？

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:57.650
<v Jen>Yeah, absolutely. Like I'll pay double.
<v Jen>是的，绝对是。比如我会付双倍的钱。

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.080
<v Ira Glass>You'll pay double.
<v Ira Glass>你会付双倍的钱。

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:08.790
<v Jen>If something's going on sale tomorrow, that's OK. You know what? I'll pay what it costs today. It's fine. Don't worry about it. I always overtip. Yeah.
<v Jen>如果明天有什么东西打折，那没关系。你知道吗？我愿意付今天的原价。没关系。别担心。我总是给很多小费。是的。

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:14.890
<v Ira Glass>I can't tell, hearing you talk about this, do you wish you were more like your mom?
<v Ira Glass>听你这么说，我看不出来，你希望自己更像你妈妈吗？

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:36.390
<v Jen>In a lot of ways, I really do. Because she's fearless. And sometimes I think, like when I'm working, if I had my mother's steel stomach, I would probably be much better at what I do. Or conversely, she would be really good at what I do. But I just don't have it in me.
<v Jen>在很多方面，我真的希望。因为她无所畏惧。有时我想，比如我在工作时，如果我有我妈妈那样的铁石心肠，我可能会做得更好。或者反过来，她会很擅长我的工作。但我身上没有那种特质。

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:48.360
<v Ira Glass>Well, after our interview, since Jen said that she wished that she were more like her mom, we got the idea to have Jen's mom give her a little lesson, a training exercise in being her. Here it is. Here's Jen.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，在我们的采访之后，既然珍说她希望自己更像妈妈，我们就想到了让珍的妈妈给她上一课，做一个关于“如何成为她”的训练练习。就在这儿。这是珍。

00:13:48.360 --> 00:14:07.830
<v Jen>We decided to try something simple. My assignment was to return a bright yellow sweater my boyfriend had received as a gift four years ago. It was from a high-end clothing store called Burberry. It was the type of sweater that might have been appropriate on a public golf course in Scotland in the mid-'80s. Suffice it to say, I hated the sweater.
<v Jen>我们决定尝试一些简单的东西。我的任务是退掉我男朋友四年前收到的一件亮黄色毛衣。那是从一家叫博柏利的高端服装店买的。那种毛衣大概在80年代中期苏格兰的公共高尔夫球场上穿还合适。足以说明，我讨厌这件毛衣。

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:27.010
<v Jen>This is what we had working for us. The sweater had originally been purchased at Burberry and had a Burberry label. There was even paper folded into the sweater, printed with the trademark logo. On the downside, we had no receipt and no hangtag. And of course, the sweater was four years out of fashion and looked it, none of which fazed my mother.
<v Jen>这就是我们手里有的筹码。这件毛衣最初确实是在博柏利买的，有博柏利的标签。毛衣里甚至还折着一张印有商标的纸。不利的一面是，我们没有收据，也没有吊牌。当然，这件毛衣已经过时四年了，看起来就很老旧，但我妈对此一点都不在意。

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:42.630
<v Jen>In fact, she thought the whole thing so easy, she decided to up the ante. She wanted me to try for a cash refund instead of a store credit. She was going to show me how.
<v Jen>事实上，她觉得整件事太简单了，决定增加赌注。她要我尝试退回现金而不是商店抵用券。她要展示给我看怎么做。

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:46.090
<v Jen>I don't have your skill, I don't know.
<v Jen>我没有你的技能，我不知道。

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:47.940
<v Sheila>I would have preferred the raincoat.
<v Sheila>我本来更想要那件雨衣的。

00:14:47.940 --> 00:15:03.510
<v Jen>My mom showed up at my office wearing a crisply pressed blouse, gigantic Jackie O sunglasses, and a pair of knockoff Burberry plaid pants that simultaneously embodied her two biggest beliefs in life. Always dress the part, and never pay full price. The training session started with some role-playing.
<v Jen>我妈出现在我的办公室，穿着一件挺括的衬衫，戴着巨大的杰奎琳·肯尼迪式的太阳镜，还有一条仿制的博柏利格子裤，同时体现了她一生中最大的两个信念。永远穿着得体，永远不要全价购买。训练课程从角色扮演开始。

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:12.660
<v Jen>OK, so I'll be me, and you'll be the Burberry's manager. Hi, I have this sweater that my boyfriend got as a gift a couple of years ago.
<v Jen>好吧，我演我，你演博柏利的经理。嗨，我这儿有一件我男朋友几年前收到的礼物。

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:25.350
<v Sheila>I don't know that I would say a couple of years ago. I wouldn't say when it was purchased. Play dumb. He just gave it to you because you were going to be in New York and asked that you return it.
<v Sheila>我不认为我会说“几年以前”。我不会说它是什么时候买的。装傻。他只是把它给了你，因为你要去纽约，并要求你退掉它。

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:30.970
<v Jen>OK, so we'll start over again. Hi, I'd like to return this sweater.
<v Jen>好的，我们重来。嗨，我想退掉这件毛衣。

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:35.860
<v Sheila>That's good. That's good. I like that. Yes, my dear, how can I help you?
<v Sheila>那很好。那很好。我喜欢这个。是的，亲爱的，我有什么能帮你的吗？

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:45.220
<v Jen>This isn't the way my mother usually works. She believes in pure improv. But for my benefit, she concocts a backstory for me. It's astonishingly complicated.
<v Jen>这不是我妈妈通常的工作方式。她相信纯粹的即兴发挥。但为了我的利益，她编造了一个非常复杂的背景故事。

00:15:45.220 --> 00:16:09.400
<v Jen>Apparently, I live in Arkansas with my Arkansas businessman boyfriend, who got this sweater as a gift, along with a lot of other Burberry clothing. Anyone who knows him knows it's his favorite store. I'm only in town for a day, and I rarely come to New York, so I want cash, not credit. And by the way, I don't know when the sweater was purchased, but he received it recently. It's such a crazy collection of facts that I have a hard time keeping it all straight.
<v Jen>显然，我住在阿肯色州，有一个阿肯色州的商人男朋友，他把这件毛衣作为礼物收下，还有很多其他的博柏利服装。认识他的人都知道这是他最喜欢的商店。我只是进城一天，很少来纽约，所以我想要现金，而不是抵用券。顺便说一句，我不知道这件毛衣是什么时候买的，但他最近才收到。这些事实组合在一起简直太疯狂了，我很难理清头绪。

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:18.320
<v Jen>OK, so from the top, here's what I'm remembering. I live in Arkansas. My boyfriend lives in Arkansas. He's in business in Arkansas. He doesn't travel to New York. I understand that it's--
<v Jen>好的，从头开始，我记住的是：我住在阿肯色州。我男朋友住在阿肯色州。他在阿肯色州做生意。他不往返于纽约。我理解这——

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:20.160
<v Sheila>Has no occasion. Has no occasion.
<v Sheila>“没有机会”。他没有机会（来纽约）。

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.040
<v Jen>What's the importance of the phrase "has no occasion"?
<v Jen>“没有机会”这个短语的重要性是什么？

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:39.260
<v Sheila>Because it makes him sound like maybe he is a more important kind of a person, rather than some yokel. Make him sound like he's a CEO of some major company, but likes small town living and does not like the city and doesn't feel comfortable--
<v Sheila>因为这让他听起来像是一个更有身份的人，而不是什么乡巴佬。让他听起来像是某个大公司的首席执行官，但喜欢小镇生活，不喜欢大城市，也不觉得自在——

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:56.660
<v Jen>This is method scamming. We head over to Burberry's. I'm wearing a hidden microphone, and my stomach immediately starts to hurt.
<v Jen>这就是专业诈骗。我们前往博柏利。我戴着隐形麦克风，胃立刻开始疼起来。

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.410
<v Jen>OK, so I'm from Arkansas, and I'm only visiting.
<v Jen>好的，我来自阿肯色州，我只是来旅游的。

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:13.730
<v Ira Glass>Burberry is a sleek, luxe store filled with coiffed salespeople and stylish Japanese tourists. There's plenty of taupe cashmere muffling the sound system. Everything is ordered, serene. I'm directed to the manager.
<v Ira Glass>博柏利是一家光鲜亮丽的精品店，到处是打理得一丝不苟的销售人员和时髦的日本游客。大量的灰褐色羊绒毯隔绝了音响系统的声音。一切都有条不紊、静谧优雅。我被引向了经理。

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:21.050
<v Jen>I have a sweater that my boyfriend received as a gift. And we live out of state. And he asked me to return it while I'm in New York.
<v Jen>我有一件我男朋友收到的礼物毛衣。我们住在外地。他让我趁在纽约的时候把它退掉。

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:39.690
<v Ira Glass>The manager barely glances at me as he informs me of their policy. No receipt, no returns, no exceptions. I muster up the courage to try again and instantly forget every lesson my mother taught me. I volunteer information. I say things that could be used against me. I can't keep the facts of my fake story straight.
<v Ira Glass>经理几乎没看我一眼，就告知了他们的政策。没收据，不退货，无一例外。我鼓起勇气再试一次，却立刻忘记了我妈妈教我的每一课。我主动提供了信息。我说了一些可能会被用来对付我的话。我无法理清那个虚假故事的事实。

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:54.080
<v Jen>It's possible it was purchased a little while ago. I don't have a receipt. And I'm actually not even sure who he got it from. So I'm not-- yeah, I have no idea to tell you the truth. He got a whole bunch of presents. I don't know.
<v Jen>它可能是前一阵子买的。我没有收据。老实说，我甚至不确定他从谁手里得到的。所以我不是——是的，说实话我一点头绪都没有。他收了一大堆礼物。我不知道。

00:17:54.080 --> 00:18:04.930
<v Ira Glass>All I want to do is get out of there. The guy is, after all, just doing his job. No receipt, no returns, no exception. I nod feebly and wonder why we don't just give the sweater to Goodwill.
<v Ira Glass>我只想尽快离开那里。毕竟，那个人只是在做他的工作。没收据，不退货，无一例外。我无力地点点头，心想为什么我们不直接把毛衣捐给慈善机构算了。

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:20.870
<v Ira Glass>I can feel that it's killing my mom to see me struggle like this, but she's trying to hold back. Finally the manager says, look, for all he knows, the sweater wasn't even bought at this store. It could have just come from some outlet. And that does it. My mom, unable to contain herself any longer, steps in.
<v Ira Glass>我能感觉到我妈妈看到我这样挣扎简直要崩溃了，但她试图忍住。最后经理说，看，据他所知，这件毛衣甚至可能不是在这家店买的。它可能只是来自某个奥特莱斯。这下触动了开关。我妈再也忍不下去了，插话道。

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:28.940
<v Sheila>Oh, I can assure you that this was not purchased at an outlet. I don't know about outlets, because I'm not an outlet shopper.
<v Sheila>哦，我可以向你保证，这绝对不是在奥特莱斯买的。我不知道奥特莱斯，因为我不是奥特莱斯的购物者。

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:49.240
<v Jen>My mom saying she's not an outlet shopper is like saying she's not an air breather. My mom spends all of five minutes dealing with the manager before deciding it's a waste of time. Her cardinal rule is if you want something done right, you have to go straight to the top. Positioning herself firmly in front of the store's only cash register, she pulls out her cell phone and calls Burberry corporate headquarters.
<v Jen>我妈说她不是奥特莱斯购物者，就像说她不呼吸空气一样。我妈花了整整五分钟应付经理，然后决定这是浪费时间。她的至理名言是，如果你想把事情办好，就必须直接找最高层。她坚定地站在商店唯一的收银台前，掏出手机给博柏利的公司总部打电话。

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:54.940
<v Sheila>Hi. I'm sorry to bother you. I'm trying to help my daughter's boyfriend.
<v Sheila>嗨。很抱歉打扰你。我想帮助我女儿的男朋友。

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:58.270
<v Jen>I want to climb into the revolving rack of raincoats and disappear.
<v Jen>我想爬进雨衣的旋转货架里消失。

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:05.300
<v Sheila>And then when he heard that my daughter was coming to New York, he asked her to bring it back to any one of the Burberry stores.
<v Sheila>然后当他听说我女儿要来纽约时，他让她把这件东西带到任何一家博柏利店。

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:12.070
<v Jen>The lies fell from her like notes in an improvisational jazz saxophone solo. She's on a tear, and she will stop at nothing.
<v Jen>谎言像即兴萨克斯独奏中的音符一样从她嘴里流出。她火力全开，绝不退缩。

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:23.800
<v Sheila>No, he received a whole bunch of gifts from Burberry. It happens to be his favorite store. And your manager can tell you that I'm wearing one of your pairs of pants today coincidentally.
<v Sheila>不，他收到了一大堆博柏利礼物。碰巧这是他最喜欢的店。你的经理可以告诉你，我今天正好穿的就是你们的裤子。

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:34.790
<v Jen>Also coincidentally, people are starting to clear away from us. I'm sure the only thing the manager would attest to at this point is that he wants me, my mother, and her fake, Burberry, plaid pants the hell out of there.
<v Jen>同样凑巧的是，人们开始远离我们。我相信此时经理唯一能证明的就是，他希望我、我妈妈和她那条虚假的博柏利格子裤赶紧滚蛋。

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:42.730
<v Sheila>Well, he's in Arkansas. I'm here in New York with the sweater. You know, there is a time difference between here and Arkansas.
<v Sheila>嗯，他在阿肯色州。我带着毛衣在纽约。你知道，这里和阿肯色州有时差。

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:46.920
<v Jen>It's 12:00 in New York, which makes it 11:00 in Arkansas.
<v Jen>纽约是12点，阿肯色州是11点。

00:19:46.920 --> 00:20:08.850
<v Sheila>Well, I have been in many situations in my life, from Chanel to Givenchy, and I have never had a situation like this. I am a faithful Burberry shopper. Well, because I'm leaving the city today. I'm going out of state. And I probably won't be back for another year, so I was hoping--
<v Sheila>嗯，我一生中经历过很多情况，从香奈儿到纪梵希，我从没遇到过这样的情况。我是博柏利的忠实顾客。嗯，因为我今天就要离开这座城市了。我要去外州。我大概一年内都不会再回来了，所以我希望能——

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:30.860
<v Jen>She's on the phone for over 20 minutes. Burberry's return policy is designed precisely with people like my mother in mind. They just keep repeating over and over, no receipt, no returns, no exceptions. At this point, I'm surprised to find that I'm getting mad. The nerve of them. After an hour and a half, we finally leave, defeated.
<v Jen>她在电话上打了20多分钟。博柏利的退货政策正是为我妈妈这样的人设计的。他们只是不断地重复，没收据，不退货，无一例外。此时，我惊讶地发现我竟然生气了。他们胆子真大。一个半小时后，我们终于离开了，失败而归。

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:48.760
<v Jen>I think you did really well, Mom. I couldn't have-- I gave up. I was dying inside. I was definitely like, oh, God. Just get us out of here. I don't want this mean man looking at me like this anymore. I feel ridiculous. I don't know what-- I'm trying to feel what a person from Arkansas would be feeling. I'm no help at all.
<v Jen>我觉得你做得很好，妈妈。我无法——我放弃了。我内心在受折磨。我当时肯定在想，哦，天哪。快带我们离开这儿。我不希望这个刻薄的人再这样看着我了。我觉得自己太荒唐了。我不知道什么——我正努力感受一个阿肯色州的人会是什么心情。我一点帮不上忙。

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:59.860
<v Sheila>I really think that since everything jived, the label-- they didn't have a hangtag. Well, maybe that's the way they wrap their gifts, without a hangtag.
<v Sheila>我真的认为既然一切都对上了，标签——他们没吊牌。嗯，也许他们送礼就是这样，不带吊牌。

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:20.370
<v Jen>Hear that? That's why I think my mom is so good at this. She truly believed she was in the right. She was actually furious at Burberry for not falling for her lies. She tells me this is unheard of. They've just lost a valuable customer. Never mind that she was never an actual customer in the first place. Later, back at the office, she chalks up our failure to one small strategic mistake.
<v Jen>听到了吗？这就是为什么我觉得我妈这么擅长此道。她真的相信她是正义的一方。她实际上对博柏利没掉进她的谎言陷阱感到愤怒。她告诉我这闻所未闻。他们刚刚失去了一位尊贵的顾客。别管她其实根本就不是什么顾客。后来，回到办公室，她把我们的失败归结为一个小小的战略失误。

