<v Jen>I think so too. I think you pushed it there. I think that's where we lost our credibility. It was a good try, Mom. It was a good effort.
<v Jen>我也这么认为。我觉得你在那一点上推得太过了。我认为那就是我们失去可信度的地方。是个很好的尝试，妈妈。已经是很努力了。

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:58.920
<v Ira Glass>What started as a lark for me, a little mother-daughter bonding time, escalated far beyond that for her. It was now a question of honor. She shot off letters to the CEO, the CFO, and, to leave no stone unturned, the mayor of New York. She refused to read these letters to me, and I'm terrified to imagine what she said.
<v Ira Glass>对我来说，这最初只是一场恶作剧，一点母女联络感情的时间，但对她来说，这远不止于此。这现在成了一个荣誉问题。她给首席执行官、首席财务官发去了信件，为了不遗漏任何细节，甚至还给纽约市长写了信。她拒绝把这些信读给我听，我害怕想象她说了些什么。

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:21.860
<v Ira Glass>They must have been some letters though. The salesman called a week later to say that they were still working on the return. He called a few days after that to tell her that there was a credit waiting for her in the Spring Street store. They credited us for $59, which was brilliant on their part, because they paid less than the sweater was worth while getting my mother out of their hair forever. Honestly, if they paid her a dollar, she'd have declared victory.
<v Ira Glass>那些信一定很厉害。一周后，销售员打来电话说他们还在处理退货事宜。几天后他又打来电话告诉她，弹簧街店里有一笔退款在等着她。他们给了我们59美元的抵用金，这对他们来说很聪明，因为他们支付的金额比毛衣本身的价值还要低，同时也让我妈妈永远离开了他们的视线。老实说，如果他们只给她一美元，她也会宣布胜利的。

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:47.060
<v Ira Glass>The problem now is I can't go get the credit, because I'm supposed to be in Arkansas. Not only that, but the store is just three blocks from my job. I'm so afraid they'll recognize me, I try to avoid their street completely. When I can't, I cross the street, so they won't see me. I know my mom wouldn't hesitate to stroll right by.
<v Ira Glass>现在的问题是我没法去取这笔退款，因为我应该在阿肯色州。不仅如此，这家店离我的工作地点只有三个街区远。我太害怕他们认出我了，我尽量完全避开那条街。实在没办法的时候，我就过马路，这样他们就看不见我了。我知道我妈会毫不犹豫地在那儿大摇大摆地走过。

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:57.560
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, The Stereo Type. The sucker worldview is that when an opportunity presents itself, you and I should grab that opportunity. And if we do not, we are suckers.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：刻板印象。冤大头世界观认为，当机会出现时，你我都应该抓住。如果我们不抓住，我们就是冤大头。

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:16.580
<v Ira Glass>Unfortunately, this mentality could also lead us to become big suckers because, of course, there is no better mark than somebody who thinks that he is about to beat the system. There's no bigger sucker than somebody who thinks that he is about to make someone else into a sucker. Well, with that in mind, we have this little fable about suckerdom from our producer, Alex Blumberg.
<v Ira Glass>不幸的是，这种心态也可能导致我们成为真正的大冤大头，因为当然，没有什么比认为自己即将打败系统的人更好的目标了。没有什么比认为自己即将让别人成为冤大头的人更大的冤大头了。好吧，考虑到这一点，我们有一个关于冤大头的小寓言，来自我们的制作人亚历克斯·布隆伯格。

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:36.860
<v Alex Blumberg>My friend Shane has this story about being a sucker and then getting the chance to get revenge on the people who suckered him. It starts when he was just 23. He had gotten one of his first adult paychecks, which seemed huge at the time. He was wearing a new suit that he'd bought with the money. He was walking down the street, thinking, this adult life is a breeze. Never before or since did he feel that the world was such a benevolent and bountiful place.
<v Alex Blumberg>我的朋友肖恩有一个关于他自己如何成为冤大头，然后有机会报复那些坑他的人的故事。故事开始于他23岁的时候。他拿到了第一份成年人的工资，在那时看来数额巨大。他穿着那笔钱买的新西装。他走在街上，心想，这成年生活真是轻松。在此之前，他从未感到世界是一个如此慷慨和富足的地方。

00:23:36.860 --> 00:24:02.990
<v Shane Dubow>And I'm approached by a van full of young guys in their, say, 20s. And it's got a stencil on the side. And they approach me, and they're like, "Hey, buddy. You want to buy some speakers?" And these guys really have it together. They're in uniforms. They have their names on the thing. The van is stenciled with the name of some company.
<v Shane Dubow>然后，一辆坐满20多岁年轻人的面包车向我靠近。车身上有喷漆模板印的字。他们靠近我，说：“嘿，伙计。你想买些扬声器吗？”这些人真的很专业。他们穿着制服，上面印着名字。面包车上也印着某家公司的名字。

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:19.170
<v Shane Dubow>And these aren't speakers that they've just pulled out of someone's living room, and the wires are dangling. They're boxed. They're packaged. They have shipping invoices. They have a whole clipboard full of delivery sites and inventory and this.
<v Shane Dubow>这些扬声器可不是那种刚从别人客厅里拔出来、电线还在晃荡的东西。它们有盒子，有包装。它们有运输发票。他们有一个装满送货地点、库存清单的剪贴板。

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:47.930
<v Shane Dubow>And they have this whole spiel. And the spiel is, "Dude, you know, we're, like, the delivery guys. And the dudes at the loading dock put too many speakers on the truck. And if we get rid of these before we get back, nobody knows, dude. Dude, we're just trying to unload these fast." And then it's like, "These are $1,200 speakers. We'll give them to you for five bills, buddy."
<v Shane Dubow>他们还有一整套说辞。说辞是，“哥们儿，你知道，我们是送货员。装卸台上的家伙装车装多了。如果我们在回去之前把这些处理掉，没人会知道的，伙计。伙计，我们只是想快点把它们卸下来。”然后接着说，“这是1200美元的音箱。500美元给你，哥们儿。”

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:52.560
<v Alex Blumberg>Shane played along, acted like he's down, he's been through this before, he's one of them.
<v Alex Blumberg>肖恩配合着演戏，装作他很懂，他经历过这些，他是他们的一员。

00:24:52.560 --> 00:25:26.050
<v Shane Dubow>We're all dudes. We all know, we all speak that language. So I say, "Well, I want to hear them." So at this point, I'm just out of college, about two, three months. And I am living at my mother's house. And we're pretty far away from there. She lives in the suburbs. We're in the city. So I say, "Well, if you want to follow me, we can go try them out." And they're like, "All right."
<v Shane Dubow>大家都是哥们儿。我们都知道，我们都说那种语言。所以我说，“好吧，我想听听它们。”那时我刚大学毕业，大概两三个月。我住在母亲家里。离那儿很远。她住在郊区。我们在城里。所以我说，“好吧，如果你们想跟我走，我们可以去试试。”他们说，“好的。”

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:42.010
<v Shane Dubow>And so they follow me all the way back to my mother's house, which is a significant drive. And I get to my mom's house. And I'm looking at their setup, because I'm trying to think, can I plug the speakers in here to listen? And I can't. There's not enough wire to take it out of the wall.
<v Shane Dubow>于是他们一路跟到我妈妈家，那是一段不短的车程。到了我妈妈家。我看着他们的设备，因为我在想，我能在这里插上插头听听吗？但我不能。没有足够的线能把它从墙上接出来。

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:47.590
<v Shane Dubow>They follow me to the audio store. And I buy some speaker wire. And we bring it home.
<v Shane Dubow>他们跟着我去了音响店。我买了些扬声器电线。我们把它们带回家。

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:57.420
<v Shane Dubow>These guys are tenacious. If this is a scam, these guys are wasting a lot of time. It's getting harder to imagine it's a scam the longer they stick with it.
<v Shane Dubow>这些人真够执着的。如果这是一个骗局，这些人浪费的时间也太多了。他们坚持得越久，就越难想象这是一个骗局。

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:23.780
<v Shane Dubow>And I bring one of the speakers inside. And I plug it in. And it sounds, it just sounds fine. It doesn't sound one way or another. And I go out and I say, "It sounds good, I guess." And they've got it in your mind that it's going to just blow you away. And I think I even said something mildly, not critical, but just unimpressed, like, "The low end sounds a little mushy."
<v Shane Dubow>我把其中一个音箱拿进去。我插上电源。听起来，就是听起来很普通。没什么特别的。我走出来说，“我觉得听起来不错。”他们在你脑子里灌输它会让你惊艳的想法。我想我甚至说了一些轻微的，不算批评但不太感冒的话，比如，“低音听起来有点糊。”

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:38.930
<v Shane Dubow>And then they have this whole spiel, like, "If you just put in Monster wire, it'll take care of that." The Monster wire, and it's Monster wire. And I don't even know what Monster wire is, but that sounds good to me.
<v Shane Dubow>然后他们又有一套说辞，比如，“如果你接上Monster电线，就能解决这个问题。”Monster电线，就是Monster电线。我甚至不知道Monster电线是什么，但这听起来不错。

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:56.900
<v Shane Dubow>So now it comes to the negotiating part. And again, I'm thinking, "Heh. These guys don't know who they're dealing with." We go back and forth, and finally, it's like, "All right. $300." Because actually, that was the most I could take out on my cash station card.
