00:33:38.340 --> 00:34:54.310
<v Ira Glass>Shane Dubow and Alex Blumberg. Coming up, an entire nation which holds to the sucker mentality. Can you guess which nation I'm talking about? Can you guess? An answer in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>肖恩·杜博和亚历克斯·布隆伯格。接下来，是一个坚持冤大头心态的国家。你能猜到我说的是哪个国家吗？你能猜到吗？一分钟后，从芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:34:54.310 --> 00:35:11.710
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Suckers, in which we ask the question, is the world in fact a place where you either make someone else into a sucker, or you're the sucker. We've arrived at act three of our program, Act Three, Suckers in the Promised Land.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目主题是：冤大头。在节目中，我们提出了这样一个问题：世界实际上是否是一个要么把别人变成冤大头，要么你就是冤大头的地方？我们来到了节目的第三幕，第三幕：应许之地的冤大头。

00:35:11.710 --> 00:35:44.880
<v Ira Glass>Regular listeners might remember not so long ago when reporter Adam Davidson and I did some stories from Israel. And while he and I were traveling around there, Adam told me to notice how often people use the word freier, which is the Yiddish word for sucker, the Israeli word for sucker. And I have to say, it happens all the time, because the idea of being a sucker is so much more powerful in Israel than it is here. Adam put together this primer on the huge place that freierism has in Israeli life, and how it explains things you didn't even know might need explaining.
<v Ira Glass>经常收听的听众可能还记得不久前记者亚当·戴维森和我做了一些关于以色列的报道。当我和他在那里旅行时，亚当让我注意人们使用“freier”这个词的频率，这是意第绪语中“冤大头”的意思，是以色列语中“冤大头”的意思。我必须说，这种情况一直在发生，因为“成为冤大头”的想法在以色列比在这里要强大得多。亚当整理了这份入门读物，介绍了“freierism”（冤大头主义）在以色列生活中的巨大地位，以及它是如何解释那些你甚至不知道需要解释的事情的。

00:35:44.880 --> 00:36:09.930
<v Adam Davidson>There is this amazing moment that was caught on film at the Camp David Peace Accords in 2000. President Clinton is leading then-Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Barak and Palestinian Authority Chairman Yasser Arafat into some log cabin for more negotiations. At the door, Arafat opens one arm to invite Barak to go in first. Barak stands there, impishly, and extends his own arm, telling Arafat to go in first.
<v Adam Davidson>2000年戴维营和平协议中，镜头捕捉到了一个惊人的时刻。克林顿总统引导当时的以色列总理埃胡德·巴拉克和巴勒斯坦权力机构主席亚西尔·阿拉法特进入一个小木屋进行进一步谈判。在门口，阿拉法特伸出一只手臂，邀请巴拉克先请。巴拉克俏皮地站在那里，伸出自己的手臂，让阿拉法特先请。

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:27.770
<v Adam Davidson>Things degenerated quickly. And you can actually see Barak and Arafat in a sort of fake wrestle, as Barak is trying to physically force Arafat to enter the door before he does. The thing seems irreconcilable until, through the door, you see President Clinton's long arms grab both men and pull them in.
<v Adam Davidson>事情迅速恶化。你可以看到巴拉克和阿拉法特在进行一种类似假摔的搏斗，巴拉克试图在体力上强迫阿拉法特先进入门内。事情看起来不可调和，直到透过门，你看到克林顿总统的长臂抓住两个人，把他们拉了进去。

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:37.700
<v Adam Davidson>I think some Americans saw this and thought it was a good sign. These two old enemies are now friendly enough for roughhousing. But for any Israeli watching, the message was clear.
<v Adam Davidson>我想一些美国人看到了这一幕，认为这是一个好兆头。这两个老敌人现在足够友好，可以打闹了。但对于任何观看节目的以色列人来说，信息很明确。

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:50.520
<v Adam Davidson>Barak was telling Arafat, I'm in control here. I decide who goes in and who doesn't. Nobody tells me when to go in a door. Put another way, I'm not your freier. I'm not your sucker.
<v Adam Davidson>巴拉克是在告诉阿拉法特，我在这里掌权。我决定谁先进去，谁不进去。没有人能告诉我什么时候进门。换句话说，我不是你的freier。我不是你的冤大头。

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:56.150
<v Tom Segev>You constantly hear it, constantly. Don't be a freier.
<v Tom Segev>你不断地听到这个词，不断地。不要做个freier。

00:36:56.150 --> 00:37:00.060
<v Adam Davidson>This is Tom Segev, an Israeli journalist and historian.
<v Adam Davidson>这是汤姆·塞格夫，一位以色列记者和历史学家。

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:20.720
<v Tom Segev>That is the worst thing for an Israeli to be, a freier, in his own eyes, and also in the eyes of other Israelis. So never, ever be generous. Be always on guard. Somebody is out there to take what is due to you.
<v Tom Segev>对于以色列人来说，最糟糕的事情就是成为一名freier，在他们自己眼中，也在其他以色列人眼中。所以千万千万不要慷慨。时刻保持警惕。有人正准备拿走你应得的东西。

00:37:20.720 --> 00:37:37.560
<v Adam Davidson>I think it would be impossible to understand Israelis without understanding the whole notion of freierism. It is at the heart of Israeli culture, affecting how people work, how they shop, how they vote, how they think about themselves and the people around them. Freierism is everywhere.
<v Adam Davidson>我认为如果不理解整个“freierism”的概念，就不可能理解以色列人。它是以色列文化的核心，影响着人们的工作方式、购物方式、投票方式，以及他们看待自己和周围人的方式。Freierism无处不在。

00:37:37.560 --> 00:38:02.730
<v Adam Davidson>When I asked my cousin David if he'd ever been a freier, like any Israeli, he first said no. But when I pressed, he surprised me by recalling, in detail, an incident that happened years ago. He was in line to pay at a supermarket, and this woman came up and asked if she could jump in front of him because she only had a few items. He said sure. Then she wheeled over this cart filled with groceries. He realized that she had made him a freier.
<v Adam Davidson>当我问我的堂兄大卫他是否曾经做过freier时，像任何以色列人一样，他首先说没有。但当我追问时，他回忆起多年前发生的一件事，细节详尽，让我很吃惊。他在超市排队付钱，这个女人走过来问她能不能插在他前面，因为她只有几件商品。他说当然可以。然后她推过来一辆装满杂货的手推车。他意识到她让他成了一个freier。

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:12.900
<v Adam Davidson>He thinks about that woman every time he goes shopping, and he has never let anyone get in front of him since. This was years ago. Again, here's Tom Segev.
<v Adam Davidson>每次他去购物都会想到那个女人，从那以后他再也没让任何人插过队。那又是几年前的事了。再一次，这是汤姆·塞格夫。

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:45.480
<v Tom Segev>Driving is a situation where the freier instinct really comes out. Driving in Israel is really hell because we will always be afraid that if you let the other guy get ahead, you will be the freier. Why would I let him go ahead of me if I can go ahead? Why should he go ahead of me?
<v Tom Segev>开车是“freier”本能真正显露出来的情况。在以色列开车简直是地狱，因为我们总是担心如果你让别人先走，你就会成为freier。如果我能先走，为什么要让他走在我前面？为什么他要走在我前面？

00:38:45.480 --> 00:39:04.830
<v Adam Davidson>From an Israeli point of view, Jews were suckers for 2,000 years in exile, constantly being tricked and persecuted. The whole idea of Israel was to create a place where Jews were in control, where Jews would never again be freiers. And even though Israel is now a powerful state, the fear of being taken advantage of hasn't gone away.
<v Adam Davidson>从以色列人的角度来看，犹太人在流亡的2000年里一直是被欺负的冤大头，不断地被欺骗和迫害。以色列整个存在的意义就是创造一个犹太人说了算的地方，一个犹太人永远不会再成为freier的地方。尽管以色列现在是一个强大的国家，但害怕被占便宜的恐惧并没有消失。

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:16.300
<v Adam Davidson>Israelis even fear that their own Jewish government makes them all freiers. Many Israelis say you're a freier if you pay your parking tickets, if you follow traffic laws. Even if you pay your taxes, you can be a freier.
<v Adam Davidson>以色列人甚至担心他们自己的犹太政府把他们都变成了freier。许多以色列人说，如果你交了停车罚单，如果你遵守交通规则，你就是个freier。甚至如果你纳税，你也可能成为一个freier。

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:38.660
<v Tom Segev>I've seen people collecting what their dogs do on the sidewalk, even though nobody watches them. And that would probably be considered a freier, somebody who obeys the law, even though there's no policeman around.
<v Tom Segev>我见过有人清理他们狗在人行道上的排泄物，即使没人看着他们。那很可能被认为是freier，一个即使没有警察在场也遵守法律的人。

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:55.290
<v Adam Davidson>Israel has an improvisational feel that America lacks. It's a place that hasn't quite settled yet. Every price can be negotiated. Every law is up for debate. Being a freier doesn't just mean getting taken advantage of, like being a sucker does here. It means you don't get how the whole system works.
<v Adam Davidson>以色列有一种美国所缺乏的即兴感。这是一个尚未完全安定下来的地方。每一个价格都可以协商。每一条法律都可以辩论。成为一名freier并不只是意味着像我们这里的冤大头那样被占便宜。它意味着你不了解整个系统是如何运作的。

00:39:55.290 --> 00:40:22.410
<v Adam Davidson>In America, you're a sucker if you buy your stereo from some guy in a parking lot. But in Israel, it's the exact opposite. Anyone can buy a stereo in a store. You're the only one who knows that Moishe's cousin from Uzbekistan just got a shipment of off-brand stereos with Sony parts, and he's selling them out of his car in the central bus station for cheap. It's not that Israelis are obsessed with getting things cheap per se. It's just that if you don't, you're a freier.
<v Adam Davidson>在美国，如果你从停车场的一个人那里买音响，你就是冤大头。但在以色列，恰恰相反。任何人都能在店里买音响。只有你一个人知道莫伊舍的乌兹别克斯坦堂兄刚收到一批带索尼零件的杂牌音响，他正在中央巴士站把它们当白菜价卖。这并不是说以色列人本身沉迷于买便宜货。只是说如果你不这样做，你就是个freier。

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:30.440
<v Adam Davidson>I've always known that. And I've always noticed that when I get to Israel and start speaking Hebrew, my whole personality changes. I'm on guard. I'm ready to yell.
<v Adam Davidson>我一直都知道这一点。而且我一直注意到，当我到达以色列并开始说希伯来语时，我的整个人格就变了。我保持警惕。我准备大喊大叫。

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:49.980
<v Adam Davidson>But I don't think I really understood what freierism means until I was checking out of this hotel in Tel Aviv a few years ago. Because of some glitch, the hotel's computer put on my bill all these phone charges to places I've never called in my life, Poland, Uruguay, Sri Lanka. I went to the manager, and in seconds, he and I were screaming at each other.
<v Adam Davidson>但我认为我直到几年前在特拉维夫那家酒店退房时，才真正理解“freierism”意味着什么。因为某种故障，酒店的电脑把那些我一生中从未打过的电话费用都记在了我的账单上，波兰、乌拉圭、斯里兰卡。我去找经理，几秒钟内，我和他就互相咆哮起来。

00:40:49.980 --> 00:41:08.510
<v Adam Davidson>I'm yelling at him, asking how he can charge for calls I never made. He's yelling back at me. This goes on for a while, until suddenly, I realize that he and I completely agree. He doesn't want me to pay the phone charges. I don't want to pay the phone charges. What we're really fighting over is who gets to say whether or not I pay the phone charges.
<v Adam Davidson>我对他大喊大叫，问他怎么能收取我没打过的电话费用。他对我大吼大叫。这持续了一会儿，直到突然间，我意识到我和他完全一致。他不想让我付那些电话费。我不想付那些电话费。我们真正争论的是谁有权决定我是否支付这些费用。

00:41:08.510 --> 00:41:28.230
<v Adam Davidson>He's saying, "I'm the manager. You don't tell me you don't pay the bill. I tell you if you don't pay the bill." And I'm yelling back in Hebrew, "I'm the customer. You don't tell me if I pay the bill. I don't pay any bill that isn't correct." That's when I got it. We weren't fighting over money. We were fighting over who was the freier, him or me.
<v Adam Davidson>他说：“我是经理。你不能告诉我不付账单。我说了算。”我用希伯来语大声回敬：“我是顾客。你不能决定我是否付账单。我不付任何不正确的账单。”就在那时，我明白了。我们争论的不是钱。我们在争论谁才是freier，是他还是我。

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:32.880
<v Adam Davidson>(INTERVIEWER) ADAM DAVIDSON Have you been a freier recently?
<v Adam Davidson>（采访者）亚当·戴维森：你最近做过freier吗？

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.800
<v Tom Segev>Probably.
<v Tom Segev>大概做过吧。

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:37.590
<v Adam Davidson>Again, here's Tom Segev.
<v Adam Davidson>再一次，这是汤姆·塞格夫。

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:48.540
<v Tom Segev>We are all freiers. We are a nation of freiers actually. We are a nation of freiers. And that's one of the childhood diseases which we have yet to overcome.
<v Tom Segev>我们都是freier。我们实际上是一个freier的民族。我们是一个freier的民族。这是我们尚未克服的儿童疾病之一。

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:54.430
<v Adam Davidson>What do you mean, you're a nation of freiers?
<v Adam Davidson>你说的“你们是一个freier的民族”是什么意思？

00:41:54.430 --> 00:42:45.100
<v Tom Segev>I can tell you something on a more serious and on a more political level. I think that the Israeli left, the Israeli peace camp feels very much like being the freier these days. Because we have been advocating peace with the Palestinians, we are advocating compromises with the Palestinians. Very often, we have done that sitting in cafes which, hours later, have been blown up by Palestinian terrorists. So I think that many of us feel that Palestinian terrorism has made us, people of the Israeli peace camp, freiers.
<v Tom Segev>我可以从更严肃、更政治的层面上告诉你一些事情。我认为以色列左派、以色列和平阵营现在感觉非常像个freier。因为我们一直主张与巴勒斯坦人和平共处，我们主张与巴勒斯坦人妥协。我们经常在咖啡馆里这样做，而几个小时后，这些咖啡馆就被巴勒斯坦恐怖分子炸毁了。所以我认为我们很多人觉得巴勒斯坦恐怖主义把我们这些以色列和平阵营的人变成了freier。

00:42:45.100 --> 00:43:14.000
<v Adam Davidson>In fact, the whole peace process is infected with the idea of freierism. Both sides know what the final outcome is going to be, a Palestinian state next to Israel, Israel giving up some settlements in the West Bank and Gaza. But each side has to play its hand to see that it gets the absolute best deal, that they aren't taken for freiers. And of course, any peace depends on both sides trusting each other. And trusting, everyone knows, is the mark of a true freier.
<v Adam Davidson>事实上，整个和平进程都感染了“freierism”的想法。双方都知道最终结果是什么，巴勒斯坦国毗邻以色列，以色列放弃西岸和加沙的一些定居点。但每一方都必须打好自己的牌，以确保得到最好的交易，确保他们不会被当作freier。当然，任何和平都取决于双方互相信任。而大家都知道，信任是真正freier的标志。

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:15.980
<v Ira Glass>Adam Davidson in Los Angeles.
<v Ira Glass>亚当·戴维森在洛杉矶。

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:25.370
<v Ira Glass>We now move on to Act Four, Mother Sucker. We have this story about the meaning of parenthood and the prevalence of being made a sucker from Heather O'Neill.
<v Ira Glass>现在进入第四幕，母爱冤大头。我们有一个关于为人父母的意义以及被当做冤大头的普遍性的故事，来自希瑟·奥尼尔。

00:43:25.370 --> 00:43:50.290
<v Heather O'neill>When I was 18 years old, I moved out of my dad's house with a suitcase and headed downtown. And there was nothing anyone could do about it. I grew up in the smallest apartment in the world, with my dad yelling all the time. My two sisters and I all slept in a tiny room with two bunk beds and a cardboard box of clothing that we all shared. After an atrocious childhood, I was delighted to be a grown-up.
<v Heather O'neill>我18岁那年，带着一个手提箱搬出了我爸爸的家，去了市中心。没人能阻止我。我在世界上最小的公寓里长大，我爸爸总是大喊大叫。我和两个妹妹睡在一个装有两个双层床的狭小房间里，还有一个我们共用的装衣服的纸箱。在经历了惨痛的童年之后，我很高兴成为一个成年人。

00:43:50.290 --> 00:44:03.150
<v Heather O'neill>My friend, Ben, and I got an apartment. You turned on the oven at 3:00 in the afternoon if you wanted it to be hot by suppertime. We never locked the door. We'd lost all the keys.
<v Heather O'neill>我的朋友本和我租了一套公寓。如果你想在晚饭时吃到热乎的，你得在下午3点打开烤箱。我们从不锁门。我们把钥匙全弄丢了。

00:44:03.150 --> 00:44:41.520
<v Heather O'neill>After living there peacefully for a couple of months, one night, Ben brought home Jimmy. Jimmy was a 16-year-old boy that he had found at the Electric Bumbum, a club down the street. Ben said that Jimmy had been walking around the bar, asking all the women for a hug. He was carrying a little leather bag that looked like a doctor's kit that he kept his underwear in. He had just escaped from Shawbridge, a juvenile detention center that was north of the city. He had tried to go home, but his mother had moved without telling him her new address.
<v Heather O'neill>在那里平静地生活了几个月后，一天晚上，本带回了吉米。吉米是一个16岁的男孩，他在街头的Electric Bumbum俱乐部发现的。本说吉米一直在酒吧里走来走去，向所有的女性求抱抱。他随身带着一个看起来像医生工具包的小皮包，用来装内裤。他刚从城北的肖布里奇少年拘留中心逃出来。他曾试着回家，但他妈妈搬家了，没告诉他新地址。

00:44:41.520 --> 00:45:10.680
<v Heather O'neill>I could see why Ben had decided to help him out. Jimmy looked like the Little Prince. He had the biggest eyes on the planet. And he always had a surprised expression on his face. He had blond hair that was sticking straight up, and he was small for his age. He was 16, but he looked about 11 or 12. On his pale bicep, he had a homemade tattoo that said Lisa. Lisa was a half-sister he had only met once, five years before.
<v Heather O'neill>我能看出本为什么决定帮助他。吉米看起来像《小王子》。他有世界上最大的眼睛。他脸上总是带着惊讶的表情。他留着竖起来的金色头发，而且他长得显小。他16岁了，但看起来像11或12岁。在他苍白的二头肌上，有一个自制的刺青写着“丽莎”。丽莎是他五年前只见过一次的同父异母妹妹。

00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:28.460
<v Heather O'neill>He also acted like a little kid. That first night when Ben brought him into the living room, I handed him this transparent balloon with white stars on it, and Jimmy went on and on about how it was the most beautiful thing anyone had ever given him. I liked that.
<v Heather O'neill>他也像个小孩子一样行事。本第一次把他带进客厅那天晚上，我给了他这个带白色星星的透明气球，吉米不停地说这是别人送给他的最美丽的东西。我喜欢这一点。

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:39.380
<v Heather O'neill>Jimmy needed a place to stay for the night. Ben said he could sleep on the couch. So Jimmy took off his pants and folded them on the coffee table. He was wearing Spider-Man underwear.
<v Heather O'neill>吉米需要一个地方过夜。本说他可以睡沙发。于是吉米脱下裤子，叠在咖啡桌上。他穿着蜘蛛侠的内裤。

00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:53.430
<v Heather O'neill>"Heather, could you tuck me in?" he asked. I thought he was joking, seeing as he was 16, and I was 18. But he just lay there, perfectly still and straight, waiting for the blankets to be pulled up to his chin.
<v Heather O'neill>“希瑟，你能帮我掖掖被子吗？”他问。我以为他在开玩笑，毕竟他16岁，我18岁。但他只是躺在那里，一动不动，笔直地等待着被子被拉到下巴。

00:45:53.430 --> 00:46:10.970
<v Heather O'neill>So I did it. I tucked him in. What else could I do? It was silly, but it also made me feel warm inside. Suddenly, I felt very mature.
<v Heather O'neill>所以我照做了。我帮他掖好了被子。我还能做什么呢？这很傻，但也让我心里感到温暖。突然间，我觉得自己非常成熟。

00:46:10.970 --> 00:46:27.420
<v Heather O'neill>When I fantasized about having a kid, it was never about being in a relationship or being pregnant. I always imagined finding a baby in a garbage can and raising it as my own. I would do everything for that little baby and never give it up.
<v Heather O'neill>当我幻想有孩子时，从没想过要谈恋爱或怀孕。我总是幻想在垃圾桶里捡到一个婴儿，然后把它抚养长大。我会为那个小宝贝做任何事，绝不放弃。

00:46:27.420 --> 00:47:01.320
<v Heather O'neill>When I was still very little, my mother told me that she was moving to New Orleans. She said she had never been able to do fun things because she had had me when she was only 19. She said if she stayed and looked after me, she would only depress me. She said really, she was doing me a favor. She left her apartment and walked down the street with her things, saying that she was going to the airport. I found out years later that she had just moved across town.
<v Heather O'neill>我还是个小孩子时，我妈妈告诉我她要搬去新奥尔良。她说她从来没能做过有趣的事情，因为她在19岁时就有了我。她说如果她留下来照顾我，她只会让我感到沮丧。她说说实话，她是在帮我。她离开了公寓，带着行李走在街上，说她要赶飞机。几年后我才知道，她只是搬到了城对过。

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:13.940
<v Heather O'neill>The next morning, when I sat on the coffee table and asked Jimmy where he was going to go, he begged me to let him stay. I told him I didn't want to get in trouble with the law. And besides, we were broke.
<v Heather O'neill>第二天早上，当我坐在咖啡桌上问吉米要到哪里去时，他恳求我让他留下。我告诉他我不想惹法律麻烦。而且，我们身无分文。

00:47:13.940 --> 00:47:41.480
<v Heather O'neill>"You guys could be my foster parents," he pleaded. Jimmy said that once a kid in foster care is 16, he can be eligible for independent living. As long as you got any adult to come and say they wanted you to live with them, it would be OK. He said he was very envious of his friend, Juan, who had talked a women who worked at his school's cafeteria into being his foster mom.
<v Heather O'neill>“你们可以做我的寄养父母，”他恳求道。吉米说，一旦寄养家庭的孩子到了16岁，就有资格独立生活。只要有任何成年人站出来说他们希望你和他们住在一起，那就没问题。他说他很羡慕他的朋友胡安，他曾说服学校食堂的一位女士做了他的寄养妈妈。

00:47:41.480 --> 00:47:52.790
<v Heather O'neill>We had to go clear across town to visit Jimmy's social worker. It was a bleak kind of neighborhood. There seemed to be train tracks everywhere and laundry lines with no clothes on them.
<v Heather O'neill>我们不得不横穿全城去见吉米的社工。那是一个阴郁的街区。到处都是火车轨道和上面没有衣服的晾衣绳。

00:47:52.790 --> 00:48:04.260
<v Heather O'neill>There were two identical buildings next to each other at the end of a street. One of the buildings had all its windows broken. The other one was the Office for Child Welfare.
<v Heather O'neill>街尽头有两栋一模一样的建筑。其中一栋的窗户全破了。另一栋是儿童福利办公室。

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:27.030
<v Heather O'neill>Before we walked into the building, I opened my mouth, and Ben squirted some breath spray in. The social worker stared at us. "Why do you want to be Jimmy's foster parents?" she asked. "I like to read books," I said. It was all I could think of to say. I'd always imagined that kids who were read to must be much smarter than me.
<v Heather O'neill>我们走进大楼之前，我张开嘴，本喷了一些口喷剂。社工盯着我们。“你们为什么要成为吉米的寄养父母？”她问。“我喜欢读书，”我说。我能想到的只有这个。我一直觉得读书的孩子一定比我聪明得多。

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:44.310
<v Heather O'neill>I was just quiet after that, but she didn't seem to care how we were planning to raise Jimmy. In fact, the only thing she wanted from us was proof that we were 18. Nothing else seemed to matter. Taking the long subway ride out there seemed to show enough initiative on our parts.
<v Heather O'neill>那之后我一直很安静，但她似乎不在乎我们打算怎么抚养吉米。事实上，她唯一想要的是我们已经18岁的证明。其他什么都不重要。搭乘那么长时间的地铁去那里似乎足以显示我们的主动性。

00:48:44.310 --> 00:49:13.370
<v Heather O'neill>She gave us an address of a school that would accept Jimmy, and then she gave us a roll of green children's bus tickets so that he could get there each day. Then she gave us some forms to fill out, which she apologized profusely for. She explained it was a drag, but it had to be done for accounting, so that we could get money. "If you have any problems, don't call me. Call the front desk first," she said, waving goodbye.
<v Heather O'neill>她给了我们一所接收吉米的学校的地址，然后给了我们一卷绿色儿童公交票，这样他每天就能去学校了。然后她给了我们一些表格要填，她为此反复道歉。她解释说这很烦人，但为了会计必须完成，这样我们才能拿到钱。“如果有什么问题，别打给我。先打给前台，”她一边挥手告别一边说。

00:49:13.370 --> 00:49:32.810
<v Heather O'neill>We were given a $100 check to buy him things that he needed. It seemed like a lot of money to me. I took him out shopping for school supplies. We got a large purse with a rainbow on it that he could use to keep his school books in. I also bought him an umbrella and a pair of snow pants.
<v Heather O'neill>我们收到了一张100美元的支票，用来给他买需要的东西。对我来说那看起来是一大笔钱。我带他去买学校用品。我们买了一个带彩虹的大钱包，他可以用来装课本。我还给他买了一把伞和一条雪裤。

00:49:32.810 --> 00:50:31.600
<v Heather O'neill>Then Jimmy and I saw this dress shirt with pink birds on it that looked like it would fit either of us perfectly. We both grabbed for it. I shoved him right into a plastic container of shoes. "Child abuse," he screamed. On the subway back home, he wouldn't stop bugging me, so I bit him on the ear. My dad used to always do that to me with his broken tooth. Pretty soon after Jimmy moved in, things started to go missing around the apartment. If we left a handful of change on the bathroom sink, it would disappear. So would Ben's cigarettes. The radio on the kitchen table was suddenly not there one day, but Jimmy denied that he had taken anything. In my heart, of course, I couldn't really believe him. But it wasn't like he was taking anything I couldn't live without.
<v Heather O'neill>然后吉米和我看到这件带粉色鸟儿的礼服衬衫，看起来穿在我们身上都很合适。我们同时伸手去抢。我把他推进了一个塑料鞋盒。“虐待儿童，”他尖叫道。在回家的地铁上，他不停地烦我，所以我咬了他的耳朵。我爸爸以前总用他那颗坏牙对我那样。吉米搬进来没多久，公寓里的东西开始丢了。如果我们把一把零钱留在浴室台子上，它就会消失。本的香烟也是。厨房桌子上的收音机突然有一天不见了，但吉米否认拿过任何东西。在我内心深处，我当然无法真正相信他。但也不是说他拿走了什么我活不下去的东西。

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:49.760
<v Heather O'neill>Jimmy had to go to a remedial high school. It wasn't long before I was being called in to talk to his teacher. She said that he kept taking his shirt off in class. She said he had 100 spelling mistakes and never did his homework. And she said that Jimmy had been missing tons of school.
<v Heather O'neill>吉米必须去一所补习高中。没过多久我就被叫去和他的老师谈话。她说他一直在课堂上脱衬衫。她说他有100个拼写错误，从不做家庭作业。而且她说吉米已经旷了很多课。

00:50:49.760 --> 00:51:00.140
<v Heather O'neill>"Why haven't you been going to school?" I asked him. "What have you been doing with your days?" "I don't know," he said.
<v Heather O'neill>“你为什么不去学校？”我问他。“这些天你在做什么？”“我不知道，”他说。

00:51:00.140 --> 00:51:18.960
<v Heather O'neill>More things vanished from the apartment, records, books, cutlery. One afternoon, while sitting outside on the stoop, I noticed that the next door neighbor was wearing Ben's hat. I asked him where he got it. And the neighbor said, "That blond kid who lives with you sold it to me."
<v Heather O'neill>更多东西从公寓消失了，唱片、书籍、餐具。一天下午，坐在门口的台阶上，我注意到隔壁邻居戴着本的帽子。我问他从哪儿弄来的。邻居说，“和你住在一起的那个金发小子卖给我的。”

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:48.920
<v Heather O'neill>Then while cleaning, I found under Jimmy's blanket a sweat sock, stuffed full, like a Christmas stocking. There was my gold chain with a pink rose. He stole a tiny porcelain southern belle that an ex-boyfriend had given to me. There was even a magnet from the fridge in the shape of a cough syrup bottle with a 1-800 number on it. When I brought it up, Jimmy said they were just souvenirs.
<v Heather O'neill>后来，在打扫卫生时，我在吉米的毯子下发现了一只装得满满的汗袜，就像圣诞长袜一样。里面有我那条带粉红玫瑰的金链子。他偷了一个前男友送给我的小瓷偶南方佳人。甚至还有一个冰箱磁铁，形状像是一个写着1-800号码的止咳糖浆瓶。当我提起这件事时，吉米说这些只是纪念品。

00:51:48.920 --> 00:52:09.980
<v Heather O'neill>I genuinely liked Jimmy, but I was slowly realizing that nobody else did. When we had friends over, he would moonwalk from one person to the next offering to massage their faces. He'd sit right next to them and ask, "If you could be any animal, what would it be? I'd be an otter because it's original and I like waterslides."
<v Heather O'neill>我真心喜欢吉米，但我慢慢意识到没别人喜欢他。当朋友们来访时，他会从一个人走到另一个人，提议给他们按摩脸部。他会坐在他们身边问，“如果你能成为任何动物，你会成为哪种？我会成为水獭，因为这很有创意，而且我喜欢水滑梯。”

00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:22.160
<v Heather O'neill>Also, he'd ask for everything. "Oh, that's a beautiful watch," he'd say. "Can I have it? Can I have your necklace? Please, I'll wear it every day."
<v Heather O'neill>而且，他会索要一切。“哦，那是个漂亮的手表，”他会说。“我能要吗？我能要你的项链吗？拜托，我会每天戴着的。”

00:52:22.160 --> 00:52:41.860
<v Heather O'neill>"Why do you let that little mooch live here?" people would ask. "He's so annoying. He's so squirrelly." I defended Jimmy, saying that he was just going through a phase. He was only a kid. It wasn't his fault that he wasn't mature. I thought he was really showing signs of an artistic temperament.
<v Heather O'neill>“你为什么要让那个小蹭吃蹭喝的住这儿？”人们会问。“他太烦人了。他像只松鼠一样。”我为吉米辩护，说他只是经历一个阶段。他还是个孩子。他不够成熟不是他的错。我认为他真的表现出艺术气质的迹象。

00:52:41.860 --> 00:53:10.420
<v Heather O'neill>After all, Rimbaud was a runaway too. If we had found Rimbaud at the Electric Bumbum, I'm sure everyone would also think he was a pretentious teenager, what with his ranting and his poetry. Rimbaud might also knock over the lamp practicing his disco moves and wear glasses with the lenses popped out in an attempt to express himself.
<v Heather O'neill>毕竟，兰波也是个离家出走者。如果我们能在Electric Bumbum找到兰波，我相信大家也会觉得他是个做作的青少年，整天在那儿狂吠和写诗。兰波练习迪斯科舞步时也可能会打翻灯，并且戴着没有镜片的眼镜来表达自我。

00:53:10.420 --> 00:53:25.160
<v Heather O'neill>We had had him six months, and still no money had come. Ben asked Jimmy to stop at the social worker and find out where the checks were. Jimmy said that he'd just been there, and that there was a paperwork problem, and we'd be getting the money soon.
<v Heather O'neill>我们养他六个月了，还是没收到钱。本让吉米去找社工看看支票在哪儿。吉米说他刚去过，有文书工作问题，我们很快就会拿到钱。

00:53:25.160 --> 00:53:56.340
<v Heather O'neill>Jimmy, our growing boy, was starting to develop a little potbelly from eating all the food in the house, while at the same time, Ben and I were starting to lose weight. Ben complained all the time about how there was never any food in the fridge. Jimmy would eat up every last thing. One time, we came home, and Jimmy was sitting on the couch, watching TV, with a big pot of spaghetti sauce on his lap. Ben started putting him on a scale to make sure he wasn't gaining weight.
<v Heather O'neill>吉米，我们那个正在长身体的孩子，因为吃光了家里所有的食物，开始长出一点小肚子，而与此同时，本和我开始消瘦。本总是在抱怨冰箱里从来没有食物。吉米会把最后一点东西吃光。有一次，我们回到家，吉米坐在沙发上看电视，腿上放着一大锅意大利面酱。本开始让他上秤，以确保他没有增加体重。

00:53:56.340 --> 00:54:15.040
<v Heather O'neill>At this point, Jimmy wasn't going to school at all. Ben started complaining that he needed to have some sort of activity, other than "eating all our food and following me around all day." "You should try to do something that involves ambition," Ben told him. "Don't you even have a hobby?"
<v Heather O'neill>此时，吉米根本不去学校。本开始抱怨说他需要做些活动，而不是“吃光我们的食物，整天跟着我”。“你应该尝试做点有志向的事情，”本告诉他。“你难道连个爱好都没有吗？”

00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:37.820
<v Heather O'neill>He decided Jimmy had to get out of the house on his own for at least an hour on the weekends. Jimmy would just stand on the balcony, shivering. He'd get down on his knees and call for me through the mail slot. I told Ben that he was pushing Jimmy too hard. When the hour was up, I would rush to unlock the door and let Jimmy back in.
<v Heather O'neill>他决定吉米周末必须至少有一个小时独自待在户外。吉米就站在阳台上颤抖。他跪在地上，通过邮件槽叫我。我告诉本他逼吉米太紧了。那一个小时结束后，我会冲去开门让吉米进来。

00:54:37.820 --> 00:55:02.660
<v Heather O'neill>Then one day, Ben had had enough. Jimmy walked in the kitchen wearing Ben's shoes, except they were painted white and had little blue stars on them. "How do you like my dancing shoes?" he asked. "Those are my shoes!" Ben screamed. "No, you never had a pair of white shoes with blue stars on them," said Jimmy. "I would have noticed."
<v Heather O'neill>后来有一天，本忍无可忍了。吉米走进厨房，穿着本的鞋子，只不过它们被涂成了白色，上面还有小蓝星星。“你觉得我的舞鞋怎么样？”他问。“那是我的鞋！”本尖叫道。“不，你从来没穿过那种带蓝星星的白鞋，”吉米说。“我会注意到的。”

00:55:02.660 --> 00:55:18.690
<v Heather O'neill>Ben told Jimmy he had gone too far. "I'm calling your social worker in the morning. I don't know how to deal with a kleptomaniac teenager."
<v Heather O'neill>本告诉吉米他太过分了。“我明天早上就打给你社工。我不知道怎么处理一个盗窃癖的青少年。”

00:55:18.690 --> 00:55:40.710
<v Heather O'neill>The thought of kicking Jimmy out made me feel rotten inside. It made me feel like a degenerate or a criminal. I couldn't stand the thought of him having no place to go. I don't know if that means I'm a sucker or that I just don't care if I'm a sucker.
<v Heather O'neill>一想到要把吉米赶走就让我内心感到烂透了。这让我觉得自己像个堕落者或罪犯。我无法忍受他无处可去。我不知道这意味着我是个冤大头，还是我根本不在乎自己是不是冤大头。

00:55:40.710 --> 00:55:54.770
<v Heather O'neill>The next morning, Jimmy was gone anyway, along with a houseload of souvenirs. He didn't even say goodbye. I thought that I had been such a good mother too. I couldn't believe he'd want to run away.
<v Heather O'neill>第二天早上，吉米还是走了，带走了一整屋的纪念品。他甚至没说再见。我还以为我一直是个这么好的母亲呢。我简直不敢相信他会想离家出走。

00:55:54.770 --> 00:56:19.580
<v Heather O'neill>When Ben woke up, he found that the envelope with his $150 that he had spent 40 hours working for was gone. The rent was due, and we were flat broke. Ben called the social worker to ask about the checks. She said that they had all been given to Jimmy. Jimmy had just been cashing them and spending the money himself.
<v Heather O'neill>本醒来时发现他辛苦工作40小时赚来的150美元的信封不见了。房租到期了，我们身无分文。本打电话给社工询问支票的事。她说它们全都给吉米了。吉米一直在兑现它们，并自己花掉了钱。

00:56:19.580 --> 00:56:53.220
<v Heather O'neill>Ben felt like Jimmy had totally taken us for fools. I felt like, well, what do you expect? Now, years later, I have a seven-year-old daughter, and I've come to realize that to be a parent is to be a sucker. You get suckered into being an unappreciated servant who works for free. You get suckered into giving up your privacy. You're suckered into giving up movies and holidays to pay for ballet lessons, and plastic alien babies, and enough bubble gum to stretch to the moon.
<v Heather O'neill>本觉得吉米把我们彻底当成了傻子。我觉得，嗯，你还指望什么？现在，几年后，我有一个七岁的女儿，我开始意识到为人父母就是做一个冤大头。你被诱骗成为一个不被赏识的、免费工作的仆人。你被诱骗放弃隐私。你被诱骗放弃电影和假日，去支付芭蕾课、塑料外星人玩具和足以拉到月球的泡泡糖的费用。

00:56:53.220 --> 00:57:07.740
<v Heather O'neill>And kids will treat you like a sucker. They'll lie right to your face. My daughter tries to convince me before leaving to school in the morning that she doesn't have lip gloss on even though her lips are glistening like candy apples.
<v Heather O'neill>而且孩子会把你当成冤大头。他们会对着你的脸撒谎。我女儿每天早上出门前都试图说服我她没擦唇彩，尽管她的嘴唇像糖苹果一样发亮。

00:57:07.740 --> 00:57:42.500
<v Heather O'neill>And what makes being a parent the ultimate sucker is that not only are you being taken for a sucker, but you actually like it. I lie and connive and manipulate on her behalf all the time, and I do it with pleasure. The other day, I sewed a little pocket inside her jacket so that she could sneak her cards to school after they were banned. "Watch out for the lunch monitors," I tell her. "They're on a power trip." I write her notes saying, "Please let my daughter go to the bathroom whenever she wants. She has serious stomach problems."
<v Heather O'neill>而且，成为父母之所以是终极的冤大头，是因为你不仅被当成了冤大头，而且你实际上很喜欢这样。我一直为她撒谎、谋划、操纵，并且乐此不疲。前几天，我给她的夹克缝了一个小口袋，好让她在卡片被禁止后能把它们偷偷带到学校。“小心午餐监察员，”我告诉她。“他们权力欲很强。”我写字条给她说，“请让我的女儿随时去厕所。她有严重的胃病。”

00:57:42.500 --> 00:58:10.380
<v Heather O'neill>And I only wish I could do more. I wish I could sit beside her during her math tests and whisper her the answers. I wish I could cup her ears with my palms when the teacher starts yelling at her for not listening. I wish I could stand with her in the schoolyard at lunchtime and give her the sandwich I packed for work because she decided to feed hers to the birds.
<v Heather O'neill>而且我只希望我能做得更多。我希望能在数学考试时坐在她身边，悄悄告诉她答案。我希望在老师开始对她大喊大叫时，用手掌捂住她的耳朵。我希望能在午饭时间站在操场上，把打包带去工作的三明治给她，因为她决定把自己的那份喂给鸟儿。

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:15.010
<v Ira Glass>Heather O'Neill is a poet and novelist.
<v Ira Glass>希瑟·奥尼尔是一位诗人和小说家。

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:27.140
<v Ira Glass>This is the last program that we have the pleasure of working with producer-in-training Chris Neary. He has had many ideas on how to structure and rework stories that have made our show better, and we are the worse for him going. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>这是我们有幸与制作实习生克里斯·尼里共事的最后一个节目。他在如何构建和重塑故事方面有很多想法，让我们的节目变得更好，而失去他则是我们的损失。《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:58:27.140 --> 00:58:30.060
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[赞助商致谢]

00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:35.340
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who's got an answer for anything we say about him here.
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责，对于我们在这里说他的任何话，他都有回应。

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:38.870
<v Shane Dubow>Oo-oo-oo-ooh. Oh, scary. Oo-oo-oo-ooh.
<v Shane Dubow>噢噢噢噢。噢，好怕怕。噢噢噢噢。

00:58:38.870 --> 00:58:56.180
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass, back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带着更多《美国生活》的故事回来。

00:58:56.180 --> 00:59:01.180
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，国际公共广播电台。
